      "side": 1,
      "message": "AI-based response:\n\nAdded — AV1E_SET_TARGET_SEQ_LEVEL_IDX(SEQ_LEVEL_KEEP_STATS) to enable the stats, then AV1E_GET_SEQ_LEVEL_IDX asserting SEQ_LEVEL_5_0, which is what 2560x1536@30 requires. Without enabling stats the control returns SEQ_LEVEL_MAX for every OP, since keep_level_stats is only set when a target level is configured (encoder.c:916).\n\nOne caveat worth flagging: this does not actually guard the change. AV1E_GET_SEQ_LEVEL_IDX → av1_get_seq_level_idx (level.c:1366) derives the level from the collected encoding statistics via check_level_constraints(); it never reads seq_params-\u003eseq_level_idx, which is the field this CL fixes. I confirmed this empirically: reverting the set_bitstream_level_tier() hunk leaves the new assertion passing.\n\nI could not find any way to observe the sequence header level through the public API — AV1E_GET_TARGET_SEQ_LEVEL_IDX returns the target, and on the decoder side parse_operating_points() (av1_dx_iface.c:234) reads seq_level_idx and discards it. For reference the values at stake are SEQ_LEVEL_2_1 (from 640x384, before the fix) versus SEQ_LEVEL_5_0 (from the forced max 2560x1536, after), so the pre-fix bitstream declares level 2.1 and then codes a 2560x1536 frame.\n\nHappy to make this a real regression test by parsing seq_level_idx[0] out of the sequence header OBU in the test (~40 lines: leb128 + OBU walk + aom_rb_read_literal), or by adding a control that exposes the header value — let me know if either is preferable to the indirect check.",
